    "abstract": "Not a few researchers of Spinoza philosophy have been fascinated by the apparent similarities between Spinoza and Freud, but have been frustrated by the opposition of their fundamental theses. I restrict my aim here to positioning their common stance and their divergence in the context of the history of modern psycho-logy, with the anticipation of more detailed subsequent research. Against to the two versions of Cartesianism in a modern psychology : the philosophy of consciousness and physiological psychology, I find the common position of Spinoza and Freud in the determinism of thoughts on the one hand, and in the conception of the human mind as a conflicted complex on the other. Indeed, Spinoza's method of deducing ideas from God rather than from the human mind corresponds to Freud's need to represent the mental apparatus by borrowing topics from anatomy. Nevertheless, the meaning of the determinism as well as the nature of complexity in the two theories are not the same. I find their fundamental difference in the ontological status of the mind and the idea. This study can serve to interpret one of Spinoza's most important propositions in its full meaning, i. e. that the human mind is a composed one, is not simple, and is a mode of thought, rather than a thinking thing.",
